1
00:00:09,732 --> 00:00:13,974
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I've heard that the same thing.

2
00:00:14,214 --> 00:00:21,598
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's what I mean when I say you don't want to have to do anything on account of suffering.

3
00:00:22,318 --> 00:00:24,059
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't want to have to manage it.

4
00:00:24,419 --> 00:00:30,522
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't want to have to do this when it arises like this, or do that when it arises in a different way.

5
00:00:30,942 --> 00:00:36,605
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to find a place, and that's what dhamma is all about, whereby you can't even suffer in the first place.

6
00:00:37,564 --> 00:00:43,329
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're not just free from any actual suffering, you're free from possibility to experience suffering.

7
00:00:43,349 --> 00:00:44,710
[SPEAKER_02]: And Nibbīrā did say similar.

8
00:00:44,910 --> 00:00:49,194
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, the dhamma doesn't free you from this unpleasant experience or that unpleasant experience.

9
00:00:49,274 --> 00:00:51,996
[SPEAKER_02]: It frees you from the nature of experience altogether.

10
00:00:53,917 --> 00:00:59,222
[SPEAKER_02]: So, again, talking about the impermanence of the stone or the tree or modern science has proven the molecules are changing.

11
00:00:59,862 --> 00:01:00,843
[SPEAKER_02]: Who cares?

12
00:01:00,863 --> 00:01:01,804
[SPEAKER_02]: It's utterly irrelevant.

13
00:01:03,988 --> 00:01:09,914
[SPEAKER_02]: You can say that, like, you know, the room is like this, the sun is like that, these are the chemicals.

14
00:01:10,014 --> 00:01:16,500
[SPEAKER_02]: It absolutely makes no difference to, like, the existential aspect of your daily life, what you feel.

15
00:01:17,543 --> 00:01:18,804
[SPEAKER_00]: It's another perception.

16
00:01:19,024 --> 00:01:22,147
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's another perception, it's another view, it's another external explanation.

17
00:01:22,768 --> 00:01:44,247
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to be honest with yourself and see whereby when there is a suffering and you start using a method to deal with that suffering, be it science, or the suttas, or psychology, or psychotherapy, or any other religion, you have to be honest with yourself to see if that method pertains to management of suffering or to actually uprooting it all together.

18
00:01:45,976 --> 00:01:51,001
[SPEAKER_02]: And you'll find out that dhamma, only dhamma, only correctly practiced, uproots the suffering.

19
00:01:52,082 --> 00:01:55,185
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything else is a management, and it's a response to suffering.

20
00:01:55,785 --> 00:01:58,047
[SPEAKER_02]: The whole psychotherapy thing, that's big in the West.

21
00:01:58,548 --> 00:02:03,212
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just managing the symptoms, not being able to cure yourself from the actual illness.

22
00:02:04,874 --> 00:02:06,835
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why I would say, people, it's wrong.

23
00:02:06,876 --> 00:02:07,796
[SPEAKER_02]: You say, well, it helps.

24
00:02:07,997 --> 00:02:11,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it helps on a level of contusion, on a mundane level.

25
00:02:12,471 --> 00:02:20,538
[SPEAKER_02]: If you were to know clearly that what you're doing is not the medicine against suffering, it's only managing the symptoms.

26
00:02:20,839 --> 00:02:31,428
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're very, very clear on it, like, you know, when you're taking a drug that can screw you up, so you're very careful, you know, okay, well, they just want this too much, so that I can go and find the proper drug.

27
00:02:32,138 --> 00:02:39,925
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, sure, okay, when the mind is weak, there's no, like, right view in sight, people will need that kind of support.

28
00:02:40,506 --> 00:02:43,449
[SPEAKER_02]: But actually, actively practicing, nobody will do it that way.

29
00:02:43,629 --> 00:02:43,809
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

30
00:02:43,849 --> 00:02:47,993
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you already have that much self-transparency, you'll find that help in the suttas already.

31
00:02:48,734 --> 00:02:57,061
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't need a Western take on, kind of, psychological quasi-medicinal, who knows what, explanation of your mental states.

32
00:02:58,782 --> 00:03:05,245
[SPEAKER_02]: But people don't see that, which is why basically... and the reason why it's wrong is because then they assume that's the practice of dhamma.

33
00:03:05,465 --> 00:03:06,026
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same.

34
00:03:07,046 --> 00:03:13,709
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why, like, lots of monks, lots of senior rajas, they go to the psychotherapy workshops and stuff, as if that contributes to their dhamma practice.

35
00:03:14,469 --> 00:03:16,750
[SPEAKER_02]: As if that can contribute to the dhamma practice.

36
00:03:17,451 --> 00:03:17,911
[SPEAKER_02]: It can't.

37
00:03:19,598 --> 00:03:21,500
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it just pertains to a completely different thing.

38
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:26,083
[SPEAKER_02]: And the worst bit about it is thinking that by doing that, you're doing dhamma.

39
00:03:26,103 --> 00:03:29,646
[SPEAKER_02]: Means, then, you won't be looking for the right cure, because you think you're having it.

40
00:03:30,928 --> 00:03:38,814
[SPEAKER_02]: If you think that management of your symptoms is the cure, actually, of your illness, you won't be looking for the right cure of your illness, because you're thinking you're curing it.

41
00:03:40,936 --> 00:03:45,680
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to be clear with yourself to recognize whether it's really curing it or is it managing it.

42
00:03:50,764 --> 00:03:52,446
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's what sīla is for.

43
00:03:53,727 --> 00:03:55,308
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, psychotherapy is not needed.

44
00:03:56,169 --> 00:03:58,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Western science is... well, not needed.

45
00:03:58,551 --> 00:03:59,692
[SPEAKER_02]: It's actually a wrong view.

46
00:04:00,673 --> 00:04:06,658
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a view that assumes the public world accessible to all of us at the same time, whereby matter is measurable.

47
00:04:07,519 --> 00:04:09,501
[SPEAKER_02]: As Buddha said, matter is always below your feet.

48
00:04:09,761 --> 00:04:10,101
[SPEAKER_02]: Never.

49
00:04:10,221 --> 00:04:11,823
[SPEAKER_02]: It can never appear in your experience.

50
00:04:12,787 --> 00:04:19,488
[SPEAKER_02]: All you're going to have is nāmarūpa, kind of nāma in relation, a designation in relation to the offered resistance.

51
00:04:20,269 --> 00:04:29,590
[SPEAKER_02]: So, things are much more subtle and much more contemplated, complicated, than taking up a ruler or doing, like, you know, some big equation in physics or whatever else.

52
00:04:30,871 --> 00:04:32,751
[SPEAKER_02]: That's explanatory.

53
00:04:32,851 --> 00:04:38,212
[SPEAKER_02]: It defines the world in its own terms, terms that are developed by science.

54
00:04:38,332 --> 00:04:40,733
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why, like, inherently, you can't use science or any other

55
00:04:42,666 --> 00:04:47,191
[SPEAKER_02]: discipline of that kind to explain your experience, because your experience precedes them.

56
00:04:47,571 --> 00:04:51,515
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, whether you're a scientist, a philosopher, or whatever, or nothing, you exist.

57
00:04:52,597 --> 00:05:03,188
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can then go and develop yourself, concoct scientific explanations, observations, and then use them to explain the tools that you used

58
00:05:03,872 --> 00:05:10,855
[SPEAKER_02]: To recognize those explanations and, in other words, you can't use your senses to access the existence of your senses.

59
00:05:11,555 --> 00:05:12,916
[SPEAKER_02]: Your eye cannot see your eye.

60
00:05:13,176 --> 00:05:14,556
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't hear your own ears.

61
00:05:16,417 --> 00:05:22,680
[SPEAKER_02]: So, relying on the measure of the sound to explain your ears, it's a mistake, because it requires your ears beforehand.

62
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:28,622
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't use it to then, like, explain its roots, because it's a result of it, not a cause of it.

63
00:05:30,303 --> 00:05:32,484
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why the only way to access these things is

64
00:05:34,027 --> 00:05:34,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Phenomenology.

65
00:05:34,967 --> 00:05:37,108
[SPEAKER_02]: And by that, I don't mean like Western philosophy.

66
00:05:37,268 --> 00:05:43,912
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean phenomena, knowledge of phenomena, phenomenology, which is what pertains to the mind, which is what dhamma is.

67
00:05:51,755 --> 00:05:57,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, within your understanding, how would you call what samādhi is?

68
00:05:58,817 --> 00:06:02,019
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I was just reading the Ajahn Chah's quote.

69
00:06:03,059 --> 00:06:04,640
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the establishment of the mind.

70
00:06:05,601 --> 00:06:07,342
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind is established properly.

71
00:06:09,663 --> 00:06:12,024
[SPEAKER_02]: And understanding is a form of samādhi.

72
00:06:13,725 --> 00:06:22,770
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why samatha and vipassana, again, preceded by the right view, not without the right view.

73
00:06:23,518 --> 00:06:25,079
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no vipassana and there is no samatha.

74
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:29,502
[SPEAKER_02]: For somebody who has a right view, samatha and vipassana go hand in hand.

75
00:06:30,302 --> 00:06:30,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Inseparable.

76
00:06:31,463 --> 00:06:33,884
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can't... Because that's one big thing.

77
00:06:33,904 --> 00:06:38,327
[SPEAKER_02]: People think, oh, there is the study and then there is a form of meditation.

78
00:06:39,567 --> 00:06:39,748
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

79
00:06:39,868 --> 00:06:41,889
[SPEAKER_02]: Your study is your meditation, if you do it right.

80
00:06:42,209 --> 00:06:44,570
[SPEAKER_02]: And your meditation is your study, if you do it right.

81
00:06:45,671 --> 00:06:49,373
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, sure, there is the aspect of gaining information, gaining knowledge.

82
00:06:49,994 --> 00:06:50,974
[SPEAKER_02]: No doubt about that.

83
00:06:51,134 --> 00:06:52,235
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's just preliminary.

84
00:06:53,455 --> 00:06:56,038
[SPEAKER_02]: What exactly do you mean by establishment of mind?

85
00:06:56,079 --> 00:07:05,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, like, say, if I try to explain you the solution to a problem, and you understand it, your mind is established upon that understanding.

86
00:07:05,531 --> 00:07:07,394
[SPEAKER_02]: You gained that understanding.

87
00:07:08,325 --> 00:07:11,948
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how is it different to, let's say, concentration?

88
00:07:12,508 --> 00:07:14,149
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's not different, no.

89
00:07:14,249 --> 00:07:15,650
[SPEAKER_02]: It requires concentration.

90
00:07:16,071 --> 00:07:23,616
[SPEAKER_02]: But the concentration is misunderstood to be that which shuts things out, as opposed to that which includes all things in.

91
00:07:25,172 --> 00:07:30,433
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the the other sutta where the Buddha gives a simile of a man who has to keep an eye on, I don't know, fifty children.

92
00:07:30,853 --> 00:07:32,834
[SPEAKER_02]: He can't trace all of them, all the time.

93
00:07:33,374 --> 00:07:36,674
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if he goes after one specifically, he loses sight of all the others.

94
00:07:37,435 --> 00:07:46,696
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he has some wisdom, so he recognizes that, and then he finds an elevated place in the yard, where he can climb up, and then from there, see all children, all the time, at once.

95
00:07:48,197 --> 00:07:51,157
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, ah, he keeps an eye on all of them, by stepping back.

96
00:07:52,158 --> 00:07:53,958
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's also from one-pointedness.

97
00:07:56,798 --> 00:08:06,185
[SPEAKER_02]: Usually, people think you focus so hard until all the other children disappear, and then you forget that you are supposed to take care of 29 children more, not just one.

98
00:08:08,847 --> 00:08:11,309
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're paying attention to the future, right?

99
00:08:11,429 --> 00:08:11,749
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

100
00:08:11,809 --> 00:08:17,654
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why the Buddha said that, like, the foundation of every practice of samādhi is mindfulness.

101
00:08:17,814 --> 00:08:21,437
[SPEAKER_02]: Every jhāna is underlined by mindfulness, like, the establishment of mindfulness.

102
00:08:22,197 --> 00:08:23,078
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's mindfulness.

103
00:08:24,445 --> 00:08:29,789
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this one has to have... must have the right view in order to attain jhānas?

104
00:08:30,430 --> 00:08:31,430
[SPEAKER_00]: No, not necessarily.

105
00:08:31,531 --> 00:08:32,271
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the thing.

106
00:08:32,331 --> 00:08:41,278
[SPEAKER_02]: See, jhānas... the practice of jhāna is along the right view, naturally, in its nature, which is why the Buddha praised it.

107
00:08:42,159 --> 00:08:45,802
[SPEAKER_02]: Theoretically, it's possible to have jhānas without having the right view.

108
00:08:46,222 --> 00:08:47,303
[SPEAKER_02]: But you would have to

109
00:08:48,145 --> 00:08:51,327
[SPEAKER_02]: Never ever hear even a single stanza of Buddhist teaching.

110
00:08:51,687 --> 00:08:52,007
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

111
00:08:52,167 --> 00:08:52,367
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

112
00:08:52,588 --> 00:08:54,368
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's it's really so close.

113
00:08:54,929 --> 00:08:56,009
[SPEAKER_02]: So Putujan can get there.

114
00:08:56,029 --> 00:08:58,871
[SPEAKER_02]: And theoretically, let's say somebody does have jhānas.

115
00:08:58,991 --> 00:08:59,771
[SPEAKER_02]: He never had a buddhism.

116
00:09:00,472 --> 00:09:11,778
[SPEAKER_02]: For him, it would probably be enough, depending on how established he is in his jhānas, just one sentence pertaining to the essence of the dhamma, and he'll become an Arya, if not, if not, if not arahant all the way.

117
00:09:12,588 --> 00:09:13,849
[SPEAKER_02]: Which were the cases in the sūtras.

118
00:09:13,869 --> 00:09:14,049
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

119
00:09:14,069 --> 00:09:17,530
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the mānas were actually established, so all of the pollutions were cleared.

120
00:09:18,230 --> 00:09:24,333
[SPEAKER_02]: They just needed the last tweak, the last kind of drop of dye that will color everything rightly.

121
00:09:25,973 --> 00:09:31,736
[SPEAKER_02]: So, but today, it's quite unlikely that a person would have jhānas and not be a sotāpanna, at least.

122
00:09:32,716 --> 00:09:37,778
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, you know, like, again, if you talk about jhānas, means you have come across Buddhist teaching, means you have read.

123
00:09:38,457 --> 00:09:42,520
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you are actually developing jhānas, you will be developing your right understanding through that.

124
00:09:43,821 --> 00:09:46,404
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's, it's, it's often whenever... What's the right meditation?

125
00:09:46,864 --> 00:09:52,569
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, first of all, in the Samādhi Sutra, they said that every aspect of the right view is right because of the right view.

126
00:09:53,469 --> 00:09:57,893
[SPEAKER_02]: Sorry, every, every aspect of the Noble Eightfold Path is right because of the right view.

127
00:09:58,834 --> 00:10:02,177
[SPEAKER_02]: So, Samasamādhi is, is right because of Samādhi.

128
00:10:02,257 --> 00:10:03,698
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything is right because of Samādhi.

129
00:10:04,879 --> 00:10:05,139
[SPEAKER_02]: So,

130
00:10:06,187 --> 00:10:09,308
[SPEAKER_02]: Provide the person has the right view, what would be the right meditation for them?

131
00:10:09,529 --> 00:10:11,049
[SPEAKER_02]: Establishing your mind through jhānas.

132
00:10:14,571 --> 00:10:15,511
[SPEAKER_02]: What is a jhāna?

133
00:10:16,792 --> 00:10:17,932
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the establishment of mind.

134
00:10:19,713 --> 00:10:23,695
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if your mind is established to such an extent... How?

135
00:10:24,616 --> 00:10:26,817
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, to a certain extent, for different...

136
00:10:27,417 --> 00:10:29,798
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not, again,

137
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:35,842
[SPEAKER_02]: Famous ānāpānasati sutta, that people teach meditation on breathing, you enter jhāna.

138
00:10:36,203 --> 00:10:36,563
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't.

139
00:10:36,863 --> 00:10:37,564
[SPEAKER_02]: Not necessarily.

140
00:10:38,484 --> 00:10:40,946
[SPEAKER_02]: You enter jhāna if you know how to enter jhāna.

141
00:10:42,026 --> 00:10:45,949
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you establish your mind in that way, if you know how to establish your mind in that way.

142
00:10:47,170 --> 00:10:49,992
[SPEAKER_02]: Ānāpānasati never mentions jhānas.

143
00:10:50,252 --> 00:10:53,414
[SPEAKER_02]: Ānāpānasati sutta doesn't talk about jhānas, talks about mindfulness.

144
00:10:54,094 --> 00:10:57,677
[SPEAKER_02]: Practicing ānāpānasati develops the four foundations of mindfulness.

145
00:10:57,697 --> 00:10:59,178
[SPEAKER_02]: The four foundations of mindfulness develop the

146
00:11:00,901 --> 00:11:01,542
[SPEAKER_02]: Four powers.

147
00:11:01,562 --> 00:11:05,243
[SPEAKER_02]: Four powers develop the seven enlightening factors.

148
00:11:05,343 --> 00:11:06,024
[SPEAKER_02]: Or something like that.

149
00:11:06,284 --> 00:11:06,944
[SPEAKER_02]: I might mix it up.

150
00:11:06,964 --> 00:11:07,424
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm tired.

151
00:11:07,864 --> 00:11:08,685
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's the point.

152
00:11:08,705 --> 00:11:09,685
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no mention of jhānas.

153
00:11:11,266 --> 00:11:19,770
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is enough to undermine the kind of common perception that you automatically enter jhāna through watching your breath.

154
00:11:20,330 --> 00:11:21,751
[SPEAKER_02]: Then I get this experience.

155
00:11:22,651 --> 00:11:23,391
[SPEAKER_02]: Or something like that.

156
00:11:23,871 --> 00:11:23,992
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

157
00:11:24,412 --> 00:11:25,652
[SPEAKER_02]: Jhāna is a stem stream of mind.

158
00:11:25,732 --> 00:11:29,134
[SPEAKER_02]: And when the Buddha did talk about it, he listed all sorts of things.

159
00:11:29,935 --> 00:11:33,197
[SPEAKER_02]: You can use it to establish your mind upon, and thus, enter jhāna.

160
00:11:33,578 --> 00:11:35,159
[SPEAKER_02]: Ānāpānasati is just one of them.

161
00:11:36,180 --> 00:11:37,260
[SPEAKER_02]: Staring at a dead body.

162
00:11:38,401 --> 00:11:41,704
[SPEAKER_02]: Staring at decomposed bodies.

163
00:11:42,584 --> 00:11:46,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Practicing ānāpānasati, and then there was... kasiṇas.

164
00:11:46,888 --> 00:11:49,249
[SPEAKER_02]: You can use kasiṇas to establish your mind in jhānas.

165
00:11:49,750 --> 00:11:52,912
[SPEAKER_02]: Eh... air, water, various things.

166
00:11:53,352 --> 00:11:59,357
[SPEAKER_02]: And then also, the suttas, whenever the suttas talk about establishment of mind, there are jhānas, then there are those

167
00:12:00,463 --> 00:12:01,143
[SPEAKER_02]: Immaterial.

168
00:12:01,664 --> 00:12:05,625
[SPEAKER_02]: They refer to them as immaterial jhānas, but they're actually immaterial establishments of mind, of a different kind.

169
00:12:06,886 --> 00:12:11,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Equally wholesome, like neither perception nor perception, and infinite space, infinite consciousness, and so on.

170
00:12:12,708 --> 00:12:17,790
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, people assume that's like the extension of the four jhānas, or they're all in the four, or almost whatever.

171
00:12:17,830 --> 00:12:20,832
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't even remember the commentaries of those schemes and divisions.

172
00:12:21,352 --> 00:12:24,393
[SPEAKER_02]: No, all of those are establishments of mind, same like ten casinos.

173
00:12:25,593 --> 00:12:29,095
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, that water, fire, air, blue, yellow,

174
00:12:34,888 --> 00:12:45,656
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the Buddha talks about them, I think only one or two suttas, but in the same way.

175
00:12:45,696 --> 00:12:47,758
[SPEAKER_02]: He establishes his mind upon the earth element.

176
00:12:48,178 --> 00:12:50,060
[SPEAKER_02]: He knows the extent of that.

177
00:12:50,560 --> 00:12:52,201
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he recognizes that establishment.

178
00:12:52,221 --> 00:12:53,402
[SPEAKER_02]: He's confident.

179
00:12:53,962 --> 00:12:59,827
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of that refined establishment, he abandons everything else that's inferior to that.

180
00:13:00,937 --> 00:13:12,287
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you look what's what's inferior to the element of worth, element of resistance, you realize, well, everything, you know, everything, particularly in your experience, is inferior to the establishment upon element of worth.

181
00:13:13,127 --> 00:13:13,387
[SPEAKER_02]: Then...

182
